From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wolfssljni: a connection the peer closed reads as end of stream

external/wolfssljni is the wolfSSL JNI/JSSE provider (Copyright (C) wolfSSL
Inc., GPL-2.0-or-later); this changes one of its files under the same
licence.

When the peer closes the TCP connection without a close_notify alert (or
resets it), native wolfSSL_read() returns 0 with SOCKET_PEER_CLOSED_E.
WolfSSLInputStream.read() tests for exactly that to report the end of the
stream, but a native library built with the OpenSSL compatibility layer
reports the error as SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL (5): the test missed, read()
returned 0, and the caller's next read() failed with
"Native wolfSSL_read() error: fatal I/O error in TLS layer (error code: 5)"
because wolfSSL refuses to read in an error state. An HTTP client thus saw
an odd I/O error where the server had simply closed the connection.

read() now takes SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL with a return value of 0 as the end of
the stream too, remembers the end (further reads return -1 without calling
into the native object), and the single-byte read() returns -1 at the end
instead of a zero byte.

---
 .../com/wolfssl/provider/jsse/WolfSSLSocket.java   | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/external/wolfssljni/src/java/com/wolfssl/provider/jsse/WolfSSLSocket.java b/external/wolfssljni/src/java/com/wolfssl/provider/jsse/WolfSSLSocket.java
index 8b4671e..85c7da7 100644
--- a/external/wolfssljni/src/java/com/wolfssl/provider/jsse/WolfSSLSocket.java
+++ b/external/wolfssljni/src/java/com/wolfssl/provider/jsse/WolfSSLSocket.java
@@ -1732,6 +1732,8 @@ public class WolfSSLSocket extends SSLSocket {
         private WolfSSLSession ssl;
         private WolfSSLSocket  socket;
         final private Object readLock = new Object();
+        /* set under readLock once the peer has ended the stream */
+        private boolean endOfStream = false;
 
         public WolfSSLInputStream(WolfSSLSession ssl, WolfSSLSocket socket) {
             this.ssl = ssl;
@@ -1754,6 +1756,11 @@ public class WolfSSLSocket extends SSLSocket {
                 throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(ioe.toString());
             }
 
+            /* end of stream is -1, not a zero byte */
+            if (ret < 0) {
+                return -1;
+            }
+
             return (data[0] & 0xFF);
         }
 
@@ -1787,6 +1794,12 @@ public class WolfSSLSocket extends SSLSocket {
                     }
                 }
 
+                /* the peer has ended the stream: it stays ended. Native
+                 * wolfSSL_read() refuses further calls with an error. */
+                if (this.endOfStream) {
+                    return -1;
+                }
+
                 /* do handshake if not completed yet, handles synchronization */
                 try {
                     /* do handshake if not completed yet, handles synchronization */
@@ -1826,9 +1839,15 @@ public class WolfSSLSocket extends SSLSocket {
                     WolfSSLDebug.log(getClass(), WolfSSLDebug.INFO,
                         "ssl.read() ret = " + ret + ", err = " + err);
 
-                    /* check for end of stream */
+                    /* check for end of stream. A peer that closes the
+                     * connection without a close_notify alert makes native
+                     * wolfSSL_read() return 0 with SOCKET_PEER_CLOSED_E; a
+                     * native library with the OpenSSL compatibility layer
+                     * reports that error as SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL. */
                     if ((err == WolfSSL.SSL_ERROR_ZERO_RETURN) ||
-                        ((err == WolfSSL.SSL_ERROR_SOCKET_PEER_CLOSED) && (ret == 0))) {
+                        ((err == WolfSSL.SSL_ERROR_SOCKET_PEER_CLOSED) && (ret == 0)) ||
+                        ((err == WolfSSL.SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL) && (ret == 0))) {
+                        this.endOfStream = true;
                         WolfSSLDebug.log(getClass(), WolfSSLDebug.INFO,
                             "ssl.read() got SSL_ERROR_ZERO_RETURN, " + err +
                             ", end of stream");
